}

page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "Navigation that fits the screen.") { Heading("Home").h1 }
page Docs(path: "/docs", title: "Docs", description: "How it works.") { Heading("Docs").h1 }
page Pricing(path: "/pricing", title: "Pricing", description: "What it costs.") { Heading("Pricing").h1 }"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("On a wide window the three links sit in a row above every page. Narrow the window and they give way to a menu button; press it and the links slide down beneath it.\n\n- [`viewport.md`](/learn/07-state-and-reactivity#the-browser-as-values) is true while the window is at least the `md` breakpoint wide. The runtime updates it when a breakpoint is crossed, so the `if` swaps the row for the button as the window narrows.\n- On a narrow screen, the `IconButton` toggles `open`. Its `label:` is the button's accessible name, since the icon has no text, and `aria-expanded: open` tells a screen reader whether the menu it controls (`aria-controls`, the `Stack`'s `id`) is open.\n- `show open` keeps the links in the page and hides them while `open` is false. `animate: .slideDown` plays as they appear, and a click on a link closes the menu again.\n- The navigation is in `app`, around the `Router`, so every page shows it and `open` lives on from page to page.\n\nThe pre-rendered page does not know the window's width, so what depends on `viewport` is drawn once the page is live. For layout alone — a row that becomes a column — prefer [responsive values](/learn/13-styling#responsive-values), which are right in the first paint.\n\nThe three pages are there so every link lands on one; in a site of your own the links name the pages you have.")

    }
}
